  "language": "en",
  "text": "Hi, good morning. Thank you for asking me here today. Ok, so my name's Steve and I work for Transport for London. Today I'm going to tell you about the work we do and in particular about the contactless ticket system we now use in London. Ok, so what do we do at Transport for London? Well, we take care of all the people who travel in the city. We look after the roads, which people use with their cars. And we look after London's public transport. People make 6 million journeys in their cars and in taxis every day and then there are millions of people who use the buses and trains. Also, of course, there's the famous London Underground, which is the name of the world's oldest metro. The London Underground carries 5 million passengers a day, so we look after a lot of people. How old is it? Sorry, what was that? How old is the London Underground? Ah, oh, it's more than 160 years old. It opened in 1863. Thanks. So, Transport for London started in 2000 and in 2003 we introduced the Oyster Card. It's a contactless ticket system. Before Oyster Cards, every passenger needed to buy a paper ticket. They bought tickets for one journey or for a day or for a month. But there was a problem. It was very slow. Why? How did people use the paper tickets? Each passenger waited to buy a ticket and then they went to the gate. At the gate, they put the ticket into the machine. Then the gate opened and they got their ticket from the machine. They needed to carry it with them until their journey finished. This took a long time and more people started to live and work in London, so we wanted a faster ticket system. And this was the Oyster Card. You can pay for your journeys online and walk through the gates much faster. You can still buy paper tickets if you want, but contactless tickets are cheaper than paper tickets. They help people save money. You can also go through the gates just by tapping your bank card on the machine and walking through. Or you can do the same with your smartphone or smartwatch. More and more people do this now in London. Around 35% of all pay-as-you-go journeys. Why? Because it's very fast and easy.",
